  "dom_id": "step-q94786",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=3{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z=t\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+8 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =3{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+8 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =-8 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line. For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-2)+8=0 \\\\ y=-2 \\\\ z=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-2,-2).$",
